    int x = INT_MAX; // maximum value for int (2147483647)
    x += 1;
    printf("%d\n", x); // prints -2147483648

    char* buf = malloc(10);
    if (!buf) {
        return 1;
    }

    // overflow occurs here
    x = -2147483648; // minimum value for int (signed)
    x += 1; // overflows to maximum value, but signed
    printf("%d\n", x); // prints 2147483647

    // heap overflow: writing past the end of the buffer
    memcpy(buf + 10, &x, sizeof(int));
    return 0;
}
```
In this example:

1. `x` overflows from its maximum value (`INT_MAX`) to its minimum value (`-2147483648`) when incremented by 1.
2. The signed integer overflow causes the value of `x` to wrap around to a large positive number (`2147483647`).
3. We then write this value past the end of the allocated buffer, causing a heap overflow.

